/**
 * @page dev_testing_sig Test signal set up
 * 
 * @if DOX_NAV
 * @ref dev_config "Back to the list of SDR device configuration sub-topics" 
 * @endif
 * 
 * @section dev_signal_set_up Configuring signal
 * 
 * A test signal can be used to generate known sample values in order to test SDR device Tx/Rx directions. For SDR devices with LMS7002M module, test signal configuration is not 
 * dependent on selected channel. Therefore, setting test signal for a Tx or Rx direction of any channel, will enable the test signal generator for all Tx or Rx directions on all
 * channels. Test signal configuration must be set by using @ref lime::SDRDevice::SetTestSignal(uint8_t, lime::TRXDir, uint8_t, lime::ChannelConfig::Direction::TestSignal, int16_t, int16_t) "SetTestSignal()"
 * function, where a configuration structure of `lime::ChannelConfig::Direction::TestSignal` type must be passed as an argument. To retrieve test signal configuration of a particular direction, function 
 * @ref lime::SDRDevice::GetTestSignal(uint8_t, lime::TRXDir, uint8_t) "GetTestSignal()" must be used.
 * 
 * Once the test signal generators for Tx direction are enabled, generated signal is outputed into physical Tx antenna ports which can be probed with appropriate equipment.
 * Once the test signal generators for Rx direction are enabled, samples are generated in a digital domain and can be read by starting the stream and reading buffered samples.
 * 
 * @subsection dev_test_sig_params Test signal parameters
 * 
 * Test signal parameters can vary depending on the SDR devices. Here is a list of @ref lime::ChannelConfig::Direction::TestSignal "test signal parameters" that are supported by SDR devices
 * with LMS7002M modules:
 * <ul>
 *    <li>Signal generator status - `Enabled` or `Disabled`</li>
 *    <li>Signal type - `DC` or `Sine`</li>
 *    <li>Signal frequency division (only for sine signal) - `8 times` or `4 times`</li>
 *    <li>Signal scale (only for sine signal) - `full` or `half`</li>
 *    <li>DC value (Read only and only for DC signal) - `I` and `Q` signal components</li>
 * </ul>
 * 
 * @subsection test_sig_example Example of test signal generation
 * 
 */